## 🎯 Interview Q&A

**Q: Why did you use Gradient Boosting over XGBoost?**
A: Scikit-learn's GradientBoostingClassifier was sufficient for this dataset size and gave clean MLflow integration. XGBoost would be the next step for larger datasets or speed requirements — same concept, optimised implementation.

**Q: What is PSI and when do you retrain?**
A: Population Stability Index measures how much a feature's distribution has shifted since training. PSI > 0.2 means significant shift — the model is seeing data it wasn't trained on. That's the retrain trigger. PSI 0.1–0.2 is a watch zone.

**Q: Why not just use accuracy as the metric?**
A: Churn rate is ~26%. A model predicting "no churn" always would get 74% accuracy while being completely useless. ROC AUC and Recall are better — AUC measures ranking quality regardless of threshold, Recall measures how many actual churners we catch.

**Q: What does hot-reload mean in the API?**
A: After retraining, you push a new .pkl file to the server and call POST /reload-model. The API loads the new model into memory without restarting the server. Users experience zero downtime during the model update.

**Q: What would you add to make this production-ready at a real company?**
A: Authentication on the API endpoints, database instead of in-memory prediction log, scheduled monitoring jobs instead of manual runs, alerting via Slack/email when drift is detected, A/B testing between model versions before full rollout, and SageMaker or Kubernetes for deployment.

